<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="ws-unit-circle" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>The Unit Circle</title>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-circle-functions" workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider a circle of radius <m>r</m> centered at the origin and a point <m>(x,y)</m> on the terminal side of an angle <m>\theta</m>.
                Then the values of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are determined by the following pair of equations:
                <md>
                    <mrow>x\amp = r\cos(\theta)</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>y\amp = r\sin(\theta)</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    <example xml:id="ex-unit-circle-1" workspace="3in">
        <p>
            Find the coordinates of the point on the terminal side of a <m>150^\circ</m> angle that lies on a circle of radius <m>5</m>.
            Then, find the value of the six trigonometric functions at this angle.
        </p>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="ex-unit-circle-2" workspace="3in">
        <p>
            Find the coordinates of the point on the terminal side of a <m>150^\circ</m> angle that lies on a circle of radius <m>1</m>.
            Then, find the value of the six trigonometric functions at this angle.
        </p>
    </example>
    <definition xml:id="def-unit-circle">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <term>unit circle</term> is a circle of radius <m>1</m> centered at the origin.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-unit-circle-trig-values">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>\theta</m> be an angle in standard position on the unit circle, and let <m>(x,y)</m> be the point on the unit circle corresponding to <m>\theta</m>.
                Then the values of the six trigonometric functions at <m>\theta</m> are given by the following equations:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sin(\theta)\amp = y\amp \csc(\theta) = \dfrac{1}{y}, y\ne 0</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>\cos(\theta)\amp = x\amp \sec(\theta) = \dfrac{1}{x}, x\ne 0</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>\tan(\theta)\amp = \dfrac{y}{x}, x\ne 0\amp \cot(\theta) = \dfrac{x}{y}, y\ne 0</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    <note>
        <title>Putting it All Together</title>
        <p>
            The unit circle is a memory device that allows us to quickly determine the values of trigonometric functions at any angle.
            In order to use the unit circle effectively for common angles, you need to know the following topics.
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Coterminal angles
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The signs of the trigonometric functions in each quadrant.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Reference angles.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The following pattern of common angles (in degrees and radians)
                        <md>
                            <mrow>0\amp\amp\frac{\pi}{6}\amp\amp\frac{\pi}{4}\amp\amp\frac{\pi}{3}\amp\amp\frac{\pi}{2}</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The following list of values both forwards and backwards.
                        <md>
                            <mrow>0\amp\amp\frac{1}{2}\amp\amp\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\amp\amp\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\amp\amp 1</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </note> 
    <example xml:id="ex-building-the-unit-circle">
        <p>
            Complete the blank unit circle given below with the common angles (in both degrees and radians) and their corresponding <m>(x,y)</m> coordinates. Use the following hints to help you complete it.
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\textbf{Hint 1:}</m> Use the table of common angles (duplicated below for your convenience) to fill in the angles in quadrant 1.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\textbf{Hint 2:}</m> Use your knowledge of reference angles and signs of the trigonometric functions to complete the information in the other quadrants.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
                    <table xml:id="table-expanded-trigonometric-functions-common-angles-repeated">
    <title>Expanded Trigonometric Functions of Common Angles</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes">
            <cell><m>\theta</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0^\circ=0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>30^\circ=\frac{\pi}{6}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>45^\circ=\frac{\pi}{4}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>60^\circ=\frac{\pi}{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>90^\circ=\frac{\pi}{2}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><m>\sin(\theta)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><m>\cos(\theta)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
</table>
    <figure xml:id="unit-circle">
        <caption>The unit circle. A line segment is drawn from the origin to the points on the circles that correspond to the multiples of <m>30^\circ</m>, <m>60^\circ</m>, and <m>90^\circ</m>. Each line segment has four blanks for students to fill in: one for the radian measure, one for the degree measure, and a pair of blanks for the coordinates on the unit circle.</caption>
        <image source="trigonometry/blank-unit-circle.png"/>
    </figure>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="ex-trig-functions-from-point" workspace="3in">
        <p>The point <m>(-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}, \frac{1}{2})</m> lies on the unit circle. Find the value of the six trigonometric functions of the angle <m>\theta</m> that is associated to this point.</p>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="ex-trig-functions-from-point-2" workspace="3in">
        <p>The point <m>(-1,0)</m> lies on the unit circle. Find the value of the six trigonometric functions of the angle <m>\theta</m> that is associated to this point.</p>
    </example>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-domain-range-sin-cos" workspace="3in">
        <title>Domain and Range of Sine and Cosine</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Sine and Cosine both have domain <m>(-\infty, \infty)</m> and range <m>[-1,1]</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-parity-trig-functions">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The functions sine, cosecant, tangent, and cotangent are odd functions. In other words,
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sin(-\theta)\amp-\sin(\theta)\amp\csc(-\theta)=-\csc(\theta)</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>\tan(-\theta)\amp-\tan(\theta)\amp\cot(-\theta)=-\cot(\theta)</mrow>
                </md>
                The functions cosine and secant are even functions. In other words,
                <md>
                    <mrow>\cos(-\theta)\amp=\cos(\theta)\amp\sec(-\theta)=\sec(\theta)</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    <example xml:id="ex-even-trig-functions" workspace="1in">
        <p>
            Find the exact value of <m>\csc(-60^\circ)</m>.
        </p>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="ex-odd-trig-functions" workspace="1in">
        <p>
            Find the exact value of <m>\tan(-\frac{\pi}{6})</m>.
        </p>
    </example>
<definition xml:id="def-periodic-function">
    <title>Periodic Functions</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            A function <m>f(x)</m> is <term>periodic</term> if there exists a positive number <m>p</m> such that <m>f(x+p)=f(x)</m> for all <m>x</m> in the domain of <m>f</m>. The smallest such positive number <m>p</m> is called the <em>period</em> of the function <m>f</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>
<theorem xml:id="thm-periodicity-sine-cosine" workspace="2in">
    <title>The Periodicity of Sine and Cosine</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The period of both the sine and cosine functions is <m>2\pi</m>. In other words, for any angle <m>\theta</m>,
            <md>
                <mrow>\sin(\theta + 2\pi)\amp=\sin(\theta)</mrow>
                <mrow></mrow>
                <mrow>\cos(\theta + 2\pi)\amp=\cos(\theta)</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>
<example xml:id="ex-periodicity-sine-cosine" workspace="3in">
    <p>
        Find the exact value of <m>\cos(\frac{11\pi}{3})</m>.
    </p>
</example>
<theorem xml:id="thm-periodicity-tan" workspace="2in">
    <title>The Periodicity of Tangent and Cotangent</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The period of both the tangent is <m>\pi</m>. In other words, for any angle <m>\theta</m>,
            <md>
                <mrow>\tan(\theta + \pi)\amp=\tan(\theta)</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>
<example xml:id="ex-periodicity-tangent" workspace="3in">
    <p>
        Find the exact value of <m>\cot(-\frac{7\pi}{4})</m>.
    </p>
</example>
<note>
    <title>Period of All Six Trigonometric Functions</title>
    <p>
        Since taking reciprocals does not change the period of a function, we know the periods of all six trigonometric functions:
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>\textbf{Sine}</m>, <m>\textbf{Cosine}</m>, <m>\textbf{Cosecant}</m>, and <m>\textbf{Secant}</m> all have period <m>2\pi</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>\textbf{Tangent}</m> and <m>\textbf{Cotangent}</m> both have period <m>\pi</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
        In particular, we can extend this result to any multiple of the period. Therefore, for any integer <m>n</m>, we have the following equations.
        <md>
            <mrow>\sin(\theta+2n\pi)\amp=\sin(\theta)\amp\csc(\theta+2n\pi)\amp=\csc(\theta)</mrow>
            <mrow></mrow>
            <mrow>\cos(\theta+2n\pi)\amp=\cos(\theta)\amp\sec(\theta+2n\pi)\amp=\sec(\theta)</mrow>
            <mrow></mrow>
            <mrow>\tan(\theta+n\pi)\amp=\tan(\theta)\amp\cot(\theta+n\pi)\amp=\cot(\theta)</mrow>
        </md>
    </p>
</note>
</worksheet>